# Cheaper monster pathfinding (O211; server/README.md "Cheaper monster pathfinding (O211)").
# Every path a creature walks to a target comes out of Map::getPathMatching (monsters chasing, summons following, a
# player's auto-walk, the Lua getPathTo calls). The search itself was most of its cost, not the tile tests: the stock
# AStarNodes zeroed 12 KB of arrays per search, put every node into a std::unordered_map (one heap allocation per
# node and per search), and found the next node to expand by scanning every node ever created. Now:
#   * src/chaospathing.h (new, header only, no engine types; server/tools/chaospathing_test.cpp and
#     server/tools/path_bench.cpp build it alone): ChaosPath::NodeStore keeps the nodes in arrays that are not cleared,
#     the node table as open addressing with a generation stamp, and the open list as a binary heap with lazy deletion
#     ordered by (f, node index), which is exactly the node the old scan picked (the lowest f, the lowest index among
#     equals). So every path, every search that fails and every node count is the same as before; the test compares the
#     two stores on 16000 random searches. One store per thread is reused (ChaosPath::Lease); a search that starts while
#     another one holds it gets a store of its own.
#   * map.h / map.cpp: AStarNodes keeps its interface and forwards to the store. Map::getPathMatching is untouched.
# Same behaviour on purpose: nothing that decides when a creature searches (Creature::onThink, goToFollowCreature,
# the 2 s refresh) or what it does with the answer changed. A search memo that skipped repeated searches was measured and
# left out (it saved 3-20% of the searches and changed how a pack reroutes; README).
